[TYP-14], [BRW-14], [TYP-5] rule 8, IV.9 (beside G1-11; MEM, critical): `=` through a `ref mut` name whose type is a base class, or `dyn I`, writes another kind into the caller's place. Program: `open class Animal:
    hp: int = 1
class Dog(Animal):
    bone: int = 7
class Cat(Animal):
    pass
fn feed(ref mut a: Animal):
    a = Cat()                 # [TYP-5] rule 8 upcasts the Cat at the assignment; [TYP-14]: `=` on a `ref mut` name writes the place it names; [BRW-14]: a `ref mut` one writes the caller's place
fn main():
    d = Dog()
    feed(ref mut d)           # a `Dog` place now holds a `Cat`
    println(d.bone)           # reads past the Cat's block: own fields follow base-class fields ([OBJ-1])`. The same with `fn bump(ref mut v: dyn Shape): v = Circle(r=1.0)` (IV.9: '`ref mut v: dyn I` may change it'), where the written value is unsized. No sentence refuses either. Rule: '* `[TYP-14a]` `=` on a `ref mut` name of a base-class or `dyn I` type never gives the place a whole value, since the place's own kind may be narrower than the name's: it is `E3023` ("`a` views a place whose kind may be narrower than `Animal`; change its fields, `a.hp = 0`, or take the object over, `owns a: Animal`, and return the new one"). A `shares mut` or `thread_shared` name of such a type is not affected: `=` on it hands the owner over ([HEAP-6]).'
